# Release ordering serial for the mobile app.
#
# THIS, NOT THE VERSION STRING, IS WHAT ORDERS RELEASES.
#
# Nuvio Z is a mod: a Nuvio Z version is a vanilla version plus a Z revision
# (0.6.0-z1, then -z2, resetting when the base moves). That number can go
# BACKWARDS by name - adopting vanilla's numbering means our first mod release
# is 0.4.9-z1 while installs are sitting on 0.4.14-beta - and the old updater
# refused anything numerically lower, so it could never have been offered.
#
# Bump this by exactly one for every published STABLE release, IN THE SAME COMMIT
# as the MARKETING_VERSION bump. The release workflow rejects any file changed
# between the version bump and the release commit, and this file is not on its
# allow-list, so a serial bumped in a later commit would fail the release.
#
# THE DEBUG LINE DELIBERATELY DOES NOT CARRY A SERIAL, and that is safe: a tag
# with no serial falls back to the string comparison, which is how the debug
# channel already orders itself, on the fourth version component.
#
# Do not "finish the job" by adding the serial to debug tags without also bumping
# it on every debug publish. Every debug build cut from one release compiles the
# SAME RELEASE_SERIAL, so two debug tags would carry equal serials, the comparison
# would find them equal, and no debug update would ever be offered - silently, with
# the version names still looking correct. The fallback is doing real work here.
#
# It is
# the same discipline CURRENT_PROJECT_VERSION already has, and it is published
# in the tag rather than as an asset:
#
#     v0.6.0-z1+127            stable, carries the serial
#     debug-v0.6.0-z1.3        debug channel, deliberately does not - see above
#
# Both updaters already read tag_name, so this costs no extra request.
# VersionUtils compares on the serial when both sides have one and falls back to
# the old string comparison when either does not, so every pre-bridge release
# stays orderable and no installed build is stranded.
#
# Seeded at 125, one above CURRENT_PROJECT_VERSION at the time (124), so a
# serial can never collide with a version code in a log or a tag.
#
# DELIBERATELY ITS OWN FILE, for the same reason DEBUG_BUILD is:
# scripts/release-metadata.sh treats any commit touching Version.xcconfig as a
# release bump and generates notes across previous_bump..current_bump, so a
# serial-only commit sitting between two releases would silently truncate the
# next release's notes. That has already happened once with the debug counter.
# See the note in DebugVersion.xcconfig.
RELEASE_SERIAL=126
